        "category": "offensive",
        "description": {
          "value": "<p>(attack, earth, magical, manipulate, range increment 120 feet) @Damage[12d6[force]], @Template[burst|distance:10], @Check[reflex|dc:30|options:area-effect]. Any nonmagical unattended stone in the burst is transformed into an equal volume of mud. Stone floors become difficult terrain; if the resulting mud is at least 3 feet deep, it is greater difficult terrain, or a creature can try to Swim through it (@Check[athletics|dc:10]). If the mud is deep enough that a creature can’t reach the bottom, they must Swim to move through it. A stone ceiling transformed into mud collapses, dealing @Damage[8d6[bludgeoning]] damage (@Check[reflex|dc:30|basic|options:area-effect] save), and spreads out, creating difficult terrain in a @Template[burst|distance:20]{20-foot radius}. If part of a stone structure is transformed into mud, the GM rolls a flat check (DC 16 for a sturdy structure, DC 14 for an average structure, DC 9 for a shoddy structure, all adjusted higher or lower as the GM sees fit). On a failure, the structure collapses, dealing @Damage[8d6[bludgeoning]] damage to each creature caught in it (@Check[reflex|dc:30|basic|options:area-effect] save) and spreads out, creating difficult terrain in the area of collapse. This mud is non-magical and remains until natural conditions cause it to dry out or otherwise disperse.</p>"
        },
